# Integrality of j(q^N) over K(j(q)) at all levels

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.isIntegral_jqNModC_all_of_modularPolynomialFamily`
(`c459801e-9abd-5df6-a5d9-10ab816f97aa`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `598effe4-7604-5a3a-a963-fcc64b10e571`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ be a field and let $N\ge 1$ be a natural number (given by a `NeZero` hypothesis). Inside the
Laurent series field `LaurentSeries K`, write $\bar\jmath =$ `jqModC K` for the Hahn series
$q^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{ofPowerSeries}(\mathtt{jNum})$, where `jNum` $=$
`eisenstein4`$^3\cdot$`dedekindEtaUnitInv` is the integral power series whose coefficients are
mapped into $K$; thus $\bar\jmath$ is the $q$-expansion of the $j$-invariant with coefficients in
$K$. For $N\ge 1$, `jqNModC K N` is the image of $\bar\jmath$ under the ring homomorphism `qExpand K
N`, which multiplies all exponents of a Laurent series by $N$, i.e. substitutes $q\mapsto q^N$. The
hypothesis `ModularPolynomialFamily` asserts that for every prime $\ell$ there exist a monic
polynomial $\Phi\in\mathbb{Z}[X][Y]$ of degree `dedekindPsi` $\ell$ in $Y$ satisfying the vanishing
relation `Φ.eval₂ evalAtJ (jqN ℓ) = 0` on $q$-expansions (the data packaged by
`ModularPolynomialData ℓ`), together with the symmetry property `EvalSymm Φ`, namely
$\Phi(x,y)=\Phi(y,x)$ for all $x,y$ in `LaurentSeries ℚ`. The conclusion is that `jqNModC K N` is
integral over the intermediate field `IntermediateField.adjoin K {jqModC K}` of $K((q))$ generated
by $\bar\jmath$ over $K$. The proof uses only the existence of the data at each prime, not the
symmetry clause `EvalSymm`.

This is the $q$-expansion form of the classical fact that $j(N\tau)$ is integral (indeed algebraic)
over the ring generated by $j(\tau)$, here deduced at all levels from the prime-level modular
equations taken as a hypothesis. It supplies the finiteness input for the modular function fields at
composite level, and is used for
[`ModularCurve.isIntegral_jqNModC_all`](thm.html#ModularCurve.isIntegral_jqNModC_all) and for the
construction of principal divisors on the Laurent base change of the full modular function field.

